rint.iacr.org/2025/343.pdf">https://eprint.iacr.org/2025/343.pdf</a>=C2=A0i=
ncludes a quick analysis of ML-KEM, saying:<br><br>&quot;Targeting 256 bits=
 of security: ML-KEM has 3 variants, targeting 128, 192, or 256 bits of sec=
urity, but all of these use 256-bit messages [Nat24]. We<br>note that the p=
arameter nc should not change for the various targeted security levels, as =
it represents a threshold on the amount of times the same public key will b=
e used for encapsulation in practical deployments. We observe that,<br>if o=
ne targets 256 bits of security, collision finding attacks become relevant =
again, since an adversary generating 2^128 ciphertexts will find a collisio=
n with one of 2^64 challenge ciphertexts with probability about 2^=E2=88=92=
64. Thus without salting, even ML-KEM-1024 can only achieve 192 bits of mul=
ti-target security in the random oracle model.&quot;<br><br>This appears to=
 indicate an upper bound of reuses for all ML-KEM parameter sets to 2^64 (i=
f I&#39;m parsing this wrong, someone correct me).=C2=A0<b></b></div><br><d=
iv class=3D"gmail_quote gmail_quote_container"><div dir=3D"ltr" class=3D"gm=
ail_attr">On Wed, Feb 25, 2026 at 4:23=E2=80=AFAM Deirdre Connolly &lt;<a h=
ref=3D"mailto:durumcrustulum@gmail.com">durumcrustulum@gmail.com</a>&gt; wr=
ote:<br></div><blockquote class=3D"gmail_quote" style=3D"margin:0px 0px 0px=
 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex"><div dir=3D=
"auto">&gt;=C2=A0My constructive suggestion is to remove all text regarding=
 static keys, as there is no reason for this draft to include them<div dir=
=3D"auto"><br></div><div dir=3D"auto">This document cannot override RFC 844=
6, which allows key reuse. We currently do not have a good citation with nu=
mbers on how many is too many reuses for ML-KEM as specified in FIPS 203 (n=
ot for generic Ring-LWE schemes as done in prior work, which ML-KEM isn&#39=
;t=E2=80=94 it is a Module-LWE scheme with the FO transform applied). We cu=
rrently gesture at any future analyses that come out.=C2=A0</div><div dir=
=3D"auto"><br></div><div dir=3D"auto">ML-KEM is of course designed to be IN=
D-CCA, but the multi-target security (specifically multi-ciphertext, or mul=
ti-challenge) is a distinct property that is relevant to a reused keypair, =
where the collision-resistsnce in the KEM/PKE=C2=A0 message space is pertin=
ent. <a href=3D"https://eprint.iacr.org/2025/343.pdf" target=3D"_blank">htt=
ps://eprint.iacr.org/2025/343.pdf</a> has some nice analysis of such securi=
ty of salted-FO KEMs (Ml-KEM is not salted) but also include a quick analys=
is of ML-KEM, saying:</div><div dir=3D"auto"><br></div><div dir=3D"auto">&q=
uot;Targeting 256 bits of security: ML-KEM has 3 variants, targeting 128, 1=
92, or 256 bits of security, but all of these use 256-bit messages [Nat24].=
 We</div><div dir=3D"auto">note that the parameter nc should not change for=
 the various targeted security levels, as it represents a threshold on the =
amount of times the same public key will be used for encapsulation in pract=
ical deployments. We observe that,</div><div dir=3D"auto">if one targets 25=
6 bits of security, collision finding attacks become relevant again, since =
an adversary generating 2^128 ciphertexts will find a collision with one of=
 2^64 challenge ciphertexts with probability about 2^=E2=88=9264. Thus with=
out salting, even ML-KEM-1024 can only achieve 192 bits of multi-target sec=
urity in the random oracle model.&quot;</div><div dir=3D"auto"><br></div><d=
iv dir=3D"auto">This appears to indicate an upper bound of reuses for all M=
L-KEM parameter sets to 2^64 (if I&#39;m parsing this wrong someone correct=
 me).=C2=A0</div><div dir=3D"auto"><br></div><div dir=3D"auto"><b>Do people=
 want to omit any mention of key reuse, implicitly relying on 8446, or incl=
ude this bounds analysis in some succinct way?</b></div></div><br><div clas=
s=3D"gmail_quote"><div dir=3D"ltr" class=3D"gmail_attr">On Wed, Feb 25, 202=
6, 2:31=E2=80=AFAM John Mattsson &lt;john.mattsson=3D<a href=3D"mailto:40er=
icsson.com@dmarc.ietf.org" target=3D"_blank">40ericsson.com@dmarc.ietf.org<=
/a>&gt; wrote:<br></div><blockquote class=3D"gmail_quote" style=3D"margin:0=
px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex">



<div>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal" style=3D"direction:ltr;text-align:left;text-indent:0=
px;text-transform:none;margin:0cm">
<span style=3D"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif;font-size:12pt;color:rgb(0,0,0)=
">Hi</span></p>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal" style=3D"text-align:left;text-indent:0px;text-transf=
orm:none;margin:0cm">
<span style=3D"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif;font-size:12pt;color:rgb(0,0,0)=
">=C2=A0</span></p>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal" style=3D"text-align:left;text-indent:0px;text-transf=
orm:none;margin:0cm">
<span style=3D"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif;font-size:12pt;color:rgb(0,0,0)=
">I received an offline comment from a senior IETF participant suggesting t=
hat I had misunderstood the draft and that the key reuse in question referr=
ed to reuse within a single
 session. That is not accurate. The draft talks about static keys, i.e., ke=
ys used in more than one key-establishment.</span></p>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal" style=3D"text-align:left;text-indent:0px;text-transf=
orm:none;margin:0cm">
<span style=3D"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif;font-size:12pt;color:rgb(0,0,0)=
">=C2=A0</span></p>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal" style=3D"text-align:left;text-indent:0px;text-transf=
orm:none;margin:0cm">
<span style=3D"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif;font-size:12pt;color:rgb(0,0,0)=
">Given the apparent confusion between 1. reuse of ephemeral keys within a =
session, 2. reuse of ephemeral keys across sessions, and 3. static keys, ea=
ch of which carries distinct
 privacy and security implications, I recommend that the AD ensure that all=
 not yet published drafts use very precise terminology. Specifically, a key=
 used in more than one key-establishment should use the well-established te=
rm =E2=80=9Cstatic key=E2=80=9D.</span></p>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal" style=3D"text-align:left;text-indent:0px;text-transf=
orm:none;margin:0cm">
<span style=3D"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif;font-size:12pt;color:rgb(0,0,0)=
">=C2=A0</span></p>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal" style=3D"text-align:left;text-indent:0px;text-transf=
orm:none;margin:0cm">
<span style=3D"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif;font-size:12pt;color:rgb(0,0,0)=
">---</span></p>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal" style=3D"text-align:left;text-indent:0px;text-transf=
orm:none;margin:0cm">
<span style=3D"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif;font-size:12pt;color:rgb(0,0,0)=
">=C2=A0</span></p>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal" style=3D"text-align:left;text-indent:0px;text-transf=
orm:none;margin:0cm">
<span style=3D"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif;font-size:12pt;color:rgb(0,0,0)=
">1. Reuse of ephemeral keys within a session:</span></p>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal" style=3D"text-align:left;text-indent:0px;text-transf=
orm:none;margin:0cm">
<span style=3D"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif;font-size:12pt;color:rgb(0,0,0)=
">=C2=A0</span></p>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal" style=3D"text-align:left;text-indent:0px;text-transf=
orm:none;margin:0cm">
<span style=3D"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif;font-size:12pt;color:rgb(0,0,0)=
">- Session A: ClientHello with MLKEM768 KeyShareEntry1 and X25519MLKEM768 =
KeyShareEntry2 both containing the same ML-KEM public key.</span></p>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal" style=3D"text-align:left;text-indent:0px;text-transf=
orm:none;margin:0cm">
<span style=3D"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif;font-size:12pt;color:rgb(0,0,0)=
">=C2=A0</span></p>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal" style=3D"text-align:left;text-indent:0px;text-transf=
orm:none;margin:0cm">
<span style=3D"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif;font-size:12pt;color:rgb(0,0,0)=
">2. Reuse of ephemeral keys across sessions:</span></p>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal" style=3D"text-align:left;text-indent:0px;text-transf=
orm:none;margin:0cm">
<span style=3D"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif;font-size:12pt;color:rgb(0,0,0)=
">=C2=A0</span></p>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal" style=3D"text-align:left;text-indent:0px;text-transf=
orm:none;margin:0cm">
<span style=3D"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif;font-size:12pt;color:rgb(0,0,0)=
">- Session B1. ClientHello with MLKEM768 KeyShareEntry3 and X25519 KeyShar=
eEntry4. Sever picks X25519 KeyShareEntry4.</span></p>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal" style=3D"text-align:left;text-indent:0px;text-transf=
orm:none;margin:0cm">
<span style=3D"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif;font-size:12pt;color:rgb(0,0,0)=
">=C2=A0</span></p>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal" style=3D"text-align:left;text-indent:0px;text-transf=
orm:none;margin:0cm">
<span style=3D"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif;font-size:12pt;color:rgb(0,0,0)=
">- Session B2. ClientHello with MLKEM768 KeyShareEntry3 and X25519 KeyShar=
eEntry5.</span></p>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal" style=3D"text-align:left;text-indent:0px;text-transf=
orm:none;margin:0cm">
<span style=3D"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif;font-size:12pt;color:rgb(0,0,0)=
">=C2=A0</span></p>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal" style=3D"text-align:left;text-indent:0px;text-transf=
orm:none;margin:0cm">
<span style=3D"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif;font-size:12pt;color:rgb(0,0,0)=
">3. Static keys</span></p>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal" style=3D"text-align:left;text-indent:0px;text-transf=
orm:none;margin:0cm">
<span style=3D"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif;font-size:12pt;color:rgb(0,0,0)=
">=C2=A0</span></p>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal" style=3D"text-align:left;text-indent:0px;text-transf=
orm:none;margin:0cm">
<span style=3D"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif;font-size:12pt;color:rgb(0,0,0)=
">- Session C1. ClientHello with MLKEM768 KeyShareEntry6. Sever picks MLKEM=
768 KeyShareEntry6</span></p>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal" style=3D"text-align:left;text-indent:0px;text-transf=
orm:none;margin:0cm">
<span style=3D"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif;font-size:12pt;color:rgb(0,0,0)=
">=C2=A0</span></p>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal" style=3D"text-align:left;text-indent:0px;text-transf=
orm:none;margin:0cm">
<span style=3D"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif;font-size:12pt;color:rgb(0,0,0)=
">- Session C2. ClientHello with MLKEM768 KeyShareEntry6. Sever picks MLKEM=
768 KeyShareEntry6</span></p>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal" style=3D"text-align:left;text-indent:0px;text-transf=
orm:none;margin:0cm">
<span style=3D"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif;font-size:12pt;color:rgb(0,0,0)=
">=C2=A0</span></p>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal" style=3D"text-align:left;text-indent:0px;text-transf=
orm:none;margin:0cm">
<span style=3D"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif;font-size:12pt;color:rgb(0,0,0)=
">---</span></p>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal" style=3D"text-align:left;text-indent:0px;text-transf=
orm:none;margin:0cm">
<span style=3D"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif;font-size:12pt;color:rgb(0,0,0)=
">=C2=A0</span></p>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal" style=3D"text-align:left;text-indent:0px;text-transf=
orm:none;margin:0cm">
<span style=3D"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif;font-size:12pt;color:rgb(0,0,0)=
">Issues with the text on static keys were clearly raised during the first =
WGLC and should have been addressed before the second. Having to repeat the=
 same issues is both frustrating
 and a waste of time.<br>
<br>
The major issues, that the draft lacks proper security considerations for t=
he use of static keys (they should be quite negative), and that it does not=
 explain how an implementation using static keys can be conformant with NIS=
T specifications, were raised during
 the first WGLC and have not been addressed. During the second WGLC, it was=
 identified that conformance is intrinsically linked to the patent license.=
</span></p>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal" style=3D"text-align:left;text-indent:0px;text-transf=
orm:none;margin:0cm">
<span style=3D"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif;font-size:12pt;color:rgb(0,0,0)=
">=C2=A0</span></p>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal" style=3D"text-align:left;text-indent:0px;text-transf=
orm:none;margin:0cm">
<span style=3D"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif;font-size:12pt;color:rgb(0,0,0)=
">My constructive suggestion is to remove all text regarding static keys, a=
s there is no reason for this draft to include them. I do not believe compa=
risons with draft-ietf-tls-hybrid-design
 are relevant, since that document serves a completely different purpose an=
d does not register code points.</span></p>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal" style=3D"text-align:left;text-indent:0px;text-transf=
orm:none;margin:0cm">
<span style=3D"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif;font-size:12pt;color:rgb(0,0,0)=
">=C2=A0</span></p>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal" style=3D"text-align:left;text-indent:0px;text-transf=
orm:none;margin:0cm">
<span style=3D"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif;font-size:12pt;color:rgb(0,0,0)=
">If the draft intends to retain any text on static keys, NIST SP 1800-37,
<i>Addressing Visibility Challenges with TLS 1.3 within the Enterprise</i>,=
 is a useful reference regarding the negative privacy and security implicat=
ions of static keys. I do not have specific guidance on how to achieve conf=
ormance with NIST specifications
 when using static keys, but that issue must clearly be resolved.</span></p=
>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal" style=3D"text-align:left;text-indent:0px;text-transf=
orm:none;margin:0cm">
<span style=3D"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif;font-size:12pt;color:rgb(0,0,0)=
">=C2=A0</span></p>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal" style=3D"text-align:left;text-indent:0px;text-transf=
orm:none;margin:0cm">
<span style=3D"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif;font-size:12pt;color:rgb(0,0,0)=
">I do not find any text in RFC 8446 that allows the use of static keys. If=
 the TLS WG intends to explicitly permit static keys again, their use shoul=
d be explicitly negotiated,
 as done in TLS 1.2 or draft-rhrd-tls-tls13-visibility.</span></p>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal" style=3D"text-align:left;text-indent:0px;text-transf=
orm:none;margin:0cm">
<span style=3D"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif;font-size:12pt;color:rgb(0,0,0)=
">=C2=A0</span></p>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal" style=3D"text-align:left;text-indent:0px;text-transf=
orm:none;margin:0cm">
<span style=3D"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif;font-size:12pt;color:rgb(0,0,0)=
">As I have seen no response from the authors indicating that they plan to =
address this issue, I am opposed to publishing this document.</span></p>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal" style=3D"text-align:left;text-indent:0px;text-transf=
orm:none;margin:0cm">
<span style=3D"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif;font-size:12pt;color:rgb(0,0,0)=
">=C2=A0</span></p>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal" style=3D"text-align:left;text-indent:0px;text-transf=
orm:none;margin:0cm">
<span style=3D"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif;font-size:12pt;color:rgb(0,0,0)=
">Cheers,</span></p>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal" style=3D"text-align:left;text-indent:0px;text-transf=
orm:none;margin:0cm">
<span style=3D"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif;font-size:12pt;color:rgb(0,0,0)=
">John</span></p>
<div style=3D"direction:ltr;font-family:Aptos,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;fo=
nt-size:12pt;color:rgb(0,0,0)">
<br>
</div>
<div id=3D"m_-5044845397023049605m_-5050180461578459232mail-editor-referenc=
e-message-container">
<div style=3D"direction:ltr">
</div>
<div style=3D"text-align:left;padding:3pt 0in 0in;border-width:1pt medium m=
edium;border-style:solid none none;border-color:rgb(181,196,223) currentcol=
or currentcolor;font-family:Aptos;font-size:12pt;color:black">
<b>From: </b>Russ Housley &lt;<a href=3D"mailto:housley@vigilsec.com" rel=
=3D"noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">housley@vigilsec.com</a>&gt;<br>
<b>Date: </b>Thursday, 12 February 2026 at 22:38<br>
<b>To: </b>Joseph Salowey &lt;<a href=3D"mailto:joe@salowey.net" rel=3D"nor=
eferrer" target=3D"_blank">joe@salowey.net</a>&gt;<br>
<b>Cc: </b><u></u>
<div style=3D"text-align:left">
&lt;<a href=3D"mailto:tls@ietf.org" rel=3D"noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">tl=
s@ietf.org</a>&gt;, John Mattsson &lt;<a href=3D"mailto:john.mattsson@erics=
son.com" rel=3D"noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">john.mattsson@ericsson.com</a=
>&gt;<br>
<b>Subject: </b>Re: [TLS] WG Last Call: draft-ietf-tls-mlkem-05 (Ends 2026-=
02-27)<br>
<br>
</div>
<u></u></div>
<div style=3D"direction:ltr">
I support the publication of this document as an RFC.=C2=A0 I would prefer =
to have the clarity about ephemeral vs. static ML-KEM keys as posted by Joh=
n Mattsson, but I can live with it as-is.</div>
<div style=3D"direction:ltr">
<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"direction:ltr">
Russ</div>
<div style=3D"direction:ltr">
<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"direction:ltr">
<br>
</div>
<blockquote>
<div>On Feb 12, 2026, at 3:08=E2=80=AFPM, John Mattsson &lt;john.mattsson=
=3D<a href=3D"mailto:40ericsson.com@dmarc.ietf.org" rel=3D"noreferrer" targ=
et=3D"_blank">40ericsson.com@dmarc.ietf.org</a>&gt; wrote:</div>
<div style=3D"direction:ltr">
<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"direction:ltr;font-family:Aptos,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;fo=
nt-size:12pt">
Hi,</div>
<div style=3D"direction:ltr;font-family:Aptos,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;fo=
nt-size:12pt">
<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"direction:ltr;font-family:Aptos,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;fo=
nt-size:12pt">
I support publication iff all text related to =E2=80=9Ckey reuse=E2=80=9D i=
s removed. In its current form, I do not believe -07 should be published.</=
div>
<div style=3D"direction:ltr;font-family:Aptos,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;fo=
nt-size:12pt">
<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"font-family:Aptos,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:12pt">
Major Comments:</div>
<div style=3D"direction:ltr;font-family:Aptos,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;fo=
nt-size:12pt">
<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"font-family:Aptos,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:12pt">
- FIPS 203 states that:</div>
<div style=3D"direction:ltr;font-family:Aptos,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;fo=
nt-size:12pt">
<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"font-family:Aptos,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:12pt">
=E2=80=9Cthe licensed patents be freely available to be practiced by any im=
plementer of the ML-KEM algorithm as published by NIST.=E2=80=9D</div>
<div style=3D"direction:ltr;font-family:Aptos,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;fo=
nt-size:12pt">
<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"font-family:Aptos,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:12pt">
=E2=80=9Crequirements for the secure use of KEMs in applications, see SP 80=
0-227.=E2=80=9D</div>
<div style=3D"direction:ltr;font-family:Aptos,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;fo=
nt-size:12pt">
<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"font-family:Aptos,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:12pt">
A reused key is, by definition, a static key. SP 800-227 imposes additional=
 requirements for static keys compared to ephemeral keys. The draft does no=
t explain how an implementer can satisfy these requirements. This creates p=
otential non-conformance with NIST
 specifications.</div>
<div style=3D"direction:ltr;font-family:Aptos,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;fo=
nt-size:12pt">
<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"font-family:Aptos,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:12pt">
- =C2=A0The draft does not describe the significant security and privacy pr=
oblems associated with key reuse. IND-CCA is a theoretical property of the =
algorithm. However, the security and privacy problems=C2=A0are related to t=
he reuse of keys in the TLS 1.3 protocol in
 deployments.</div>
<div style=3D"direction:ltr;font-family:Aptos,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;fo=
nt-size:12pt">
<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"font-family:Aptos,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:12pt">
Minor Comments:</div>
<div style=3D"direction:ltr;font-family:Aptos,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;fo=
nt-size:12pt">
<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"font-family:Aptos,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:12pt">
- The discussion of randomness reuse in ciphertexts and references to SP 80=
0-227 do not belong in a =E2=80=9Ckey reuse=E2=80=9D section. Ciphertexts a=
re not keys, and SP 800-227 contains broader guidelines and requirements be=
yond static keys.</div>
<div style=3D"direction:ltr;font-family:Aptos,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;fo=
nt-size:12pt">
<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"font-family:Aptos,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:12pt">
- =E2=80=9CThe client&#39;s shares are listed in descending order of client=
 preference; the server selects one algorithm and sends its corresponding s=
hare.=E2=80=9D</div>
<div style=3D"direction:ltr;font-family:Aptos,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;fo=
nt-size:12pt">
<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"font-family:Aptos,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:12pt">
The server may also select no share and respond with a handshake_failure or=
 a HelloRetryRequest (HRR). Since this is already specified in RFC 8446, it=
 would be better to remove this text and simply reference RFC 8446.</div>
<div style=3D"direction:ltr;font-family:Aptos,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;fo=
nt-size:12pt">
<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"font-family:Aptos,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:12pt">
- Section 5.1 appears to mix different concepts: hybrids, PQ/T hybrids, and=
 lattice-based PQ/T hybrids. I assume the person asking for this section wa=
nted a comparison with [ECDHE-MLKEM]. I suggest doing that. In the future, =
PQ/T hybrids will likely become
 less common, but it is unclear whether other hybrids (e.g., ML-KEM + HQC-K=
EM) will gain adoption.</div>
<div style=3D"direction:ltr;font-family:Aptos,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;fo=
nt-size:12pt">
<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"font-family:Aptos,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:12pt">
Cheers,</div>
<div style=3D"font-family:Aptos,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:12pt">
John</div>
<div style=3D"direction:ltr;font-family:Aptos,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;fo=
nt-size:12pt">
<br>
</div>
<div id=3D"m_-5044845397023049605m_-5050180461578459232mail-editor-referenc=
e-message-container">
<div style=3D"padding:3pt 0in 0in;border-width:1pt medium medium;border-sty=
le:solid none none;border-color:rgb(181,196,223) currentcolor currentcolor"=
>
<div style=3D"text-align:left;font-family:Aptos;font-size:12pt">
<b>From: </b>Joseph Salowey &lt;<a href=3D"mailto:joe@salowey.net" rel=3D"n=
oreferrer" target=3D"_blank">joe@salowey.net</a>&gt;<br>
<b>Date: </b>Thursday, 12 February 2026 at 20:06<br>
<b>To:</b></div>
<div style=3D"text-align:left;font-family:Aptos;font-size:12pt">
&lt;<a href=3D"mailto:tls@ietf.org" rel=3D"noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">tl=
s@ietf.org</a>&gt;<br>
<b>Subject: </b>[TLS] WG Last Call: draft-ietf-tls-mlkem-05 (Ends 2026-02-2=
7)<br>
<br>
</div>
</div>
<div style=3D"direction:ltr;line-height:normal;margin:0px;font-family:&quot=
;Helvetica Neue&quot;;font-size:13px">
This message starts the second Working Group Last Call for the pure ML-KEM =
document (draft-ietf-tls-mlkem-07).</div>
<div style=3D"direction:ltr;line-height:normal;margin:0px;min-height:15px;f=
ont-family:&quot;Helvetica Neue&quot;;font-size:13px">
<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"direction:ltr;line-height:normal;margin:0px;font-family:&quot=
;Helvetica Neue&quot;;font-size:13px">
The file can be retrieved from:</div>
<div style=3D"direction:ltr;line-height:normal;margin:0px;font-family:&quot=
;Helvetica Neue&quot;;font-size:13px">
<a href=3D"https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-ietf-tls-mlkem/" style=3D=
"margin-top:0px;margin-bottom:0px" rel=3D"noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">htt=
ps://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-ietf-tls-mlkem/</a><br>
<br>
The diff with the previous WGLC draft (-05) is here:</div>
<div style=3D"direction:ltr;line-height:normal;margin:0px;min-height:15px;f=
ont-family:&quot;Helvetica Neue&quot;;font-size:13px">
<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"direction:ltr;line-height:normal;margin:0px;font-family:&quot=
;Helvetica Neue&quot;;font-size:13px">
<a href=3D"https://author-tools.ietf.org/iddiff?url1=3Ddraft-ietf-tls-mlkem=
-05&amp;url2=3Ddraft-ietf-tls-mlkem-06&amp;difftype=3D--html" style=3D"marg=
in-top:0px;margin-bottom:0px" rel=3D"noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">https://=
author-tools.ietf.org/iddiff?url1=3Ddraft-ietf-tls-mlkem-05&amp;url2=3Ddraf=
t-ietf-tls-mlkem-07&amp;difftype=3D--html</a></div>
<div style=3D"direction:ltr;line-height:normal;margin:0px;min-height:15px;f=
ont-family:&quot;Helvetica Neue&quot;;font-size:13px">
<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"direction:ltr;line-height:normal;margin:0px;font-family:&quot=
;Helvetica Neue&quot;;font-size:13px">
The main focus of this WGLC is to review new text providing more context ar=
ound the use of pure ML-KEM.=C2=A0 For those who indicated they wanted this=
 text, please let us know if the new text satisfies you and if you support =
publication. This working group last
 call will end on February 27, 2026.=C2=A0</div>
<div style=3D"direction:ltr;line-height:normal;margin:0px;min-height:15px;f=
ont-family:&quot;Helvetica Neue&quot;;font-size:13px">
<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"direction:ltr;line-height:normal;margin:0px;font-family:&quot=
;Helvetica Neue&quot;;font-size:13px">
Thank You.</div>
</div>
